Experiment 4 – Jenkins Static Website Deployment Using Docker
1. Aim
To install and configure Jenkins using Docker and create a Jenkins Pipeline to generate and publish a static website.
2. Procedure
Step 1 – Check Docker installation
Open Windows Command Prompt and execute:
docker --version
docker compose version


Step 2 – Create the project folder
mkdir jenkins-static-site
cd jenkins-static-site


Step 3 – Create Docker Compose file
notepad docker-compose.yml


Paste the following configuration into Notepad:

services:
  jenkins:
    image: jenkins/jenkins:lts
    container_name: jenkins
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
      - "50000:50000"
    volumes:
      - jenkins_home:/var/jenkins_home
    restart: unless-stopped

volumes:
  jenkins_home:



Save the file as docker-compose.yml and close Notepad.
Step 4 – Start Jenkins container
Execute:
docker compose up -d


This downloads the Jenkins image if needed and starts Jenkins in the background.
Step 5 – Verify the container
docker ps


Check that the Jenkins container is running and port 8080 is mapped.
Step 6 – Get the initial administrator password
docker exec jenkins cat /var/jenkins_home/secrets/initialAdminPassword


Copy the password displayed in CMD.
Step 7 – Open Jenkins in the browser
Open:

http://localhost:8080
1. Paste the initial administrator password.
2. Click Install suggested plugins.
3. Wait for the plugins to install.
4. Create your administrator account.
5. Continue until the Jenkins dashboard appears.
Step 8 – Install HTML Publisher plugin
1. Open Manage Jenkins.
2. Select Plugins.
3. Search for HTML Publisher.
4. Install the plugin.
5. Restart Jenkins if requested.
Step 9 – Create a Pipeline job
1. Click New Item.
2. Enter the name: karunya-site-pipeline.
3. Select Pipeline.
4. Click OK.
5. Scroll to the Pipeline section.
6. Select Pipeline script under Definition.
Step 10 – Enter the Pipeline script
Paste this complete script into the Pipeline Script box:

pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Generate site') {
            steps {
                script {
                    sh 'mkdir -p site/assets'

                    writeFile file: 'site/index.html', text: '''
                    <html>
                    <head>
                        <title>Karunya University</title>
                        <link rel="stylesheet" href="assets/style.css">
                    </head>
                    <body>
                        <h1>Welcome to Karunya University</h1>
                        <p>DevOps Lab - Jenkins Deployment Using Docker</p>
                    </body>
                    </html>
                    '''

                    writeFile file: 'site/assets/style.css', text: '''
                    body {
                        font-family: Arial;
                        text-align: center;
                        background-color: lightblue;
                        padding: 50px;
                    }
                    h1 {
                        color: darkblue;
                    }
                    '''
                }
            }
        }

        stage('Publish site') {
            steps {
                publishHTML(target: [
                    reportName: 'Karunya University - Simple Site',
                    reportDir: 'site',
                    reportFiles: 'index.html',
                    keepAll: true,
                    alwaysLinkToLastBuild: true,
                    allowMissing: false
                ])

                archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'site/**/*',
                                 fingerprint: true
            }
        }
    }
}



Step 11 – Save and execute the Pipeline
1. Click Save.
2. Click Build Now.
3. Wait for the build to finish.
4. Open the build number.
5. Select Console Output.
6. Verify that the build displays:
Finished: SUCCESS


Step 12 – Verify the published website
1. Return to the Jenkins job dashboard.
2. Find the published HTML report link.
3. Click Karunya University - Simple Site.
4. Verify that the static website is displayed.
Welcome to Karunya University
DevOps Lab - Jenkins Deployment Using Docker

Expected website output

Step 13 – Verify archived files
Open the completed build and check the Artifacts section.

Expected files:
site/index.html
site/assets/style.css


